{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People are made of cells, cells are made \nof molecules, molecules are made of atoms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6000,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoms are made of electrons and proton and neutrons, and those last two are made up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10740,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of three quarks. Simple stuff, right? \nAll except for that last part. Protons and neutrons are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17220,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually made of many, many quarks that just happen to look like three quarks when you look at them in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particular way. And even then, sometimes they’re \nmade of 5 quarks - including the charm quark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34397,
    "dDurationMs": 5263,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The humble proton may seem simple enough, \nand they’re certainly common. Most of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39660,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible matter in the universe comes from \nprotons, either as lonely hydrogen nuclei  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44820,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or bound with neutrons into the nuclei \nof the various elements of the periodic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48480,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "table. You’d think by now we’d have a pretty \ngood idea of the properties of the proton.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53160,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, their interiors remain a profound \nmystery. It wasn’t until the late 60s that we even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60060,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirmed out that protons were not themselves \nelementary particles, but consistent of 3  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65340,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarks. Now, thanks to AI we have evidence that \nsuggests they may be made of five quarks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72360,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least sometime. Two of them heavier than the proton itself. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76020,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make sense of this we need \nto understand how physicists probe the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80220,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smallest scales of nature. And that means \nunderstanding the physics of scattering.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85620,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every time you open your eyes you’re performing \na scattering experiment. Photons from some light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91260,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "source like the Sun bounce - or scatter - \noff objects in the world around you into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96780,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your particle detectors—aka your eyes. Your \nanalysis computer—aka your brain—then builds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104100,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up a colour map of the world around you \nbased on the properties of those photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108660,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it’s also possible to \n“see” the subatomic world if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112920,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we do a different type of scattering experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115920,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, the higher the energy \nthe scattering particle has, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller the object you can see. To see subatomic \nscales, it’s better not to use photons at all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead to use particles of matter. That's \nhow Earnest Rutherford first discovered the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129840,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus. He shot a beam of alpha particles \n- which we now know to be helium nuclei - at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135420,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thin gold foil. He found that, while \nmost particles passed straight through,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140460,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a small fraction scattered off something very small and very dense. That’s how he figured out that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148260,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom is mostly empty space, with most of its \nmass concentrated in a tiny central nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153720,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since Rutherford’s 1911 experiment we’ve \ngotten much better at doing this. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159300,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we now have electron microscopes which \nshoot electrons into a sample and measure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how they’re deflected. Sophisticated software \nthen reconstructs the structure of the sample,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169080,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as our brain reconstructs \nthe structure of the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172260,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electron scattering can be used to study \nthe structure of anything larger than an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178140,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron. That sounds pretty useful, because \nelectrons have no size—they’re point-like.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually not so fast—electrons are quantum \nobjects and so have a wavelength that gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189420,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shorter the more energy the electron has. \nSo really I should say that electrons can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194520,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be used to study anything that’s larger than the \nelectron’s wavelength, which depends on energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202140,
    "dDurationMs": 6602,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you want to study the interior of a \nproton you need a pretty high energy electron.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208742,
    "dDurationMs": 5158,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Below a certain energy, the electron will just \nbounce off the proton as a whole. But with enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213900,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy the electron will punch into a proton and \nthen scatter off the proton's internal parts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220260,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should add that such an electron is energetic \nenough to destroy the proton. Which sounds bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226740,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s actually okay - sometimes you need \nto break something to see what it’s made of."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230820,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s what physicists at the Stanford Linear \nAccelerator Center—SLAC—managed to do in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "50s and 60s. They accelerated electrons to higher energies than had previously been achieved and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244080,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slammed them into protons, then watched what came \nout the other side. In 1968 they published their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251220,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results. From those scattering products and the \ndeflection of the electrons, the SLAC physicists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deduced that protons must be made of three \npoint-like particles. These particles matched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the properties of the so-called quarks—theoretical \ncomponents of nucleons proposed by Murry Gell-Mann  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269880,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and George Zweig earlier that decade. And that’s \nhow we get the proton model that you’re probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276540,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "familiar with—two up and one down quark, with \nthree different charge colours bound to each other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282720,
    "dDurationMs": 6027,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by gluons carrying the strong nuclear force, \nas we’ve talked about in previous episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288747,
    "dDurationMs": 4283,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And same for the neutron except it's two down and one up. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293030,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'll be talking about protons from now on, but everything I say probably applies to neutrons too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298500,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the SLAC electron beam was \nthe most energetic of its time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301800,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we quickly figured out how to make more and \nmore powerful accelerators. And as we did so,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307020,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we started to see something strange. \nRemember I said that higher energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311220,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scattering allows us to see smaller \nthings and finer resolution. Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316800,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as we powered up our accelerators that’s exactly \nwhat we saw—the detailed guts of the proton,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322620,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it turned out to be much more complicated \nthan just three quarks bound to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328020,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of the proton was revealed to be \na complex cluster of energy—a dense network  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333480,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gluons that are constantly transforming \ninto pairs of virtual quarks and antiquarks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338880,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which quickly annihilate each other, turning \nback into gluons. We call this the quark sea,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343860,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this constantly shifting, flickering \nmess is a stormy ocean indeed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349980,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because the quantum properties of \ncharge, spin, and colour must be conserved,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355380,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is order within the chaos. If you took all \nthe contents of the quark sea in one instant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361440,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could list all the quarks and all the \nantiquarks, and see that they cancel each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366060,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other out except for two up quarks and one down \nquark. These “valence” quarks will constantly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373260,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exchange energy and colour charge via the quark \nsea and may even be annihilated by a virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379140,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle or antiparticle, but are instantly \nreplaced by that virtual particle’s partner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These valence quarks are what the first SLAC \nexperiments detected. But as we increased the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron beam’s energy, those electrons started \nto interact with the transient entities of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396240,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quark sea. They scattered off gluons and virtual \nquarks, revealing that complex inner structure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402360,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The higher the energy of the electrons, the \nmore fine detail we saw. We saw the virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407340,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and down quarks of the quark sea, the gluon \nnetwork. But also some weird stuff. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413280,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 1% of the time there was evidence of \na charm quark inside the proton. Well that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419460,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "odd. The charm quark weighs more than \nthe entire proton—36% more in fact.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426240,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like opening a 1 kg box of I dunno, \napples, and finding a 1.3 kg melon inside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435480,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately there’s a perfectly plausible \nexplanation in the case of the proton. It turns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441180,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out that the particles we discover after smashing \nan electron into a proton aren’t necessarily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446820,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside the proton to start with. That’s because \nthe energy carried by the electron can create  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452040,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brand new particles. Remember, E=mc^2, which \ntells us that mass and energy can be converted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into each other. As the ingoing electron transfers \nits substantial kinetic energy into the quark sea,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new particle-antiparticle pairs can be created \nthat were not part of the proton to start with.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the energy of the electron beam increases, \nwe get a scattering signal from finer and finer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477840,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure. But this signal is increasingly muddied \nwith new particles created in the collision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483660,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call the particles that are inside the proton \nto start with intrinsic particles, and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489120,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "include the valence quarks and the virtual quarks \nand gluons of the quark sea. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494580,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call particles created in the collision itself \nextrinsic particles. After a particle collision  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501900,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see both intrinsic and extrinsic particles, \nas well as the decay products of those particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507480,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in cases where the outgoing particle is too \nunstable to reach the detector before falling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513420,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apart. So if the ingoing electron has enough \nenergy, perhaps it could produce charm quarks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518700,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are detected in some cases. If the charm \nquarks are extrinsic then there’s no problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526320,
    "dDurationMs": 8820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even early on, there was some evidence \nof intrinsic charm quarks. There was the hint  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535140,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the presence of charm quarks even in cases \nwhere the electron didn’t carry enough energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce one. So if the charm quark is too \nmassive to be produced by the incoming electron,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547800,
    "dDurationMs": 6721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and is too massive to be a regular part \nof the entire proton, how can it exist?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554521,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it’s not quite as simple a conundrum as I \nmake it sound. As you reduce the energy of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560400,
    "dDurationMs": 5635,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the collision, the probability of finding \nan extrinsic charm quark also goes down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566035,
    "dDurationMs": 7745,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but only reaches zero at zero energy, which \nmeans when no collision is happening. In these early experiments "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573780,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charm quarks were appearing only slightly more often at low energies than expected from calculations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578520,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the evidence for intrinsic charm quarks was weak. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583620,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s talk about how those calculations and how they're done to see why this uncertainty exists.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of the proton is the realm of \nthe strong nuclear force, which is described  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595800,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by quantum chromodynamics. With its multiple \ndifferent charge types and different charge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601620,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carriers, QCD is far more complicated than say the single-charged electrodynamics. One weird thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610020,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about “QCD” is that the calculations are easier at \nhigh energy than at low energy because it’s easier  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616800,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to implement a favorite hack of quantum mechanics \ncalled perturbation theory. That means it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623220,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easier to calculate the result of a high-energy \nscattering event than a low one. It’s extra hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629220,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to properly calculate the state of the interior \nof the proton in the absence of a collision.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634560,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For this reason it's kinda straight foward to explain all the extrinsic quarks that we see at high energies, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is very difficult to explain the behavior of the \nthree intrinsic quarks we see at low energies, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's even harder to explain why we seem to find \nthese charmed quark at those same low energies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although it’s hard to model the interior of the \nproton at low energy, our intuition still tells  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658080,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us that it shouldn’t contain things more massive \nthan the proton itself—like the charm quark.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663780,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is actually a way to get quantum \nchromodynamics to give us intrinsic charm quarks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670140,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do that by taking advantage of the Heisenberg \nuncertainty principle, which, among other things "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that we can borrow energy from out of \nnowhere to create massive particle-antiparticle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682440,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as those particles \nvanish again very quickly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685980,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more massive the particles, the \nshorter they’re allowed to live.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689940,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For this reason, it should be possible \nto generate a charm-anticharm quark pair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694860,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even inside a proton, as long as it \nexists only for a very short duration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699420,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to the large mass of the charm quark pair, \ntheir momentary appearance is a bigger deal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706560,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the proton’s inner structure than is the \ncontinuous flickering of virtual up and down  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711540,
    "dDurationMs": 9180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarks. In that instance of their appearance, the proton looks more like a 5-quark particle than a 3-quark particle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However I wouldn’t go so far as to say that \nthe proton briefly more massive. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726480,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proton’s measured mass comes from the average of \nits internal energy over the time of measurement.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732840,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the charm quarks only exist \nfor a tiny fraction of the time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736860,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then they contribute only a fraction of \ntheir own hefty mass to the proton’s mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742500,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just gave a crude outline of the theory \nof intrinsic charm, proposed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748200,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stanley Brodsky in 1980 to explain the weak evidence of charm quarks in protons. But proving this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755340,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was challenging due to the difficulty of \nmodeling low-energy particle collisions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760860,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One issue is that there are typically many \ndifferent models of the proton interior  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765420,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can lead to the prediction of the same \nscattering results. So, you might come up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a model that includes intrinsic charm \nthat gives a perfect match for your average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775680,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle output over many collisions. But you \nmay have gotten that model right just by chance, and if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782700,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you analyzed even more collisions you'd realize \nthe correct model is actually totally different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787020,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without testing all possible models, it’s \nimpossible to know whether a model of intrinsic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792540,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charm was really better at predicting the outcome \nof these collisions, versus some unknown model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797940,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that doesn’t include intrinsic charm. So \nnow you have a better idea of why "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801840,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s so much uncertainty about the \nexistence of charm quarks in the proton "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806700,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "—it just wasn’t possible to sift through \nall of the possible models to be sure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811500,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that intrinsic charm was needed to \nexplain the scattering experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, until Artificial Intelligence \ncame along. This new tool allowed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822720,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scientists in the NNPDF collaboration to do \nsomething that was previously impossible:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829140,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of testing one model, they could test \nhundreds or thousands of models of the proton interior at the same time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836640,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They trained a neural network to analyze \nnearly 30 years of proton collisions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not constrained by a single model, but in the \nlimit of all possible models. The network was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847920,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then able to create new models that approach \nthe scattering data as closely as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852420,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was incredible. Instead of a few scientists \ntrying a few models every couple of years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they could have this machine test thousands \nof models in just a couple of days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863520,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and while a scientists may be biased to trying to \nto find evidence in favor of their favorite model,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870120,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the network just wants to find the best \nanswer, even if it contradicts intrinsic charm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876180,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now we get to the punchline. This \nNeural Network was able to find a model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880980,
    "dDurationMs": 5953,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with intrinsic charm that predicts the \ndata much better than any previous model.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886933,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now currently this is still tentative. The team reports a 3-sigma result which means a 1 in 1000 chance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893743,
    "dDurationMs": 5443,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they found the wrong model in favor of intrinsic charm just by random chance. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899186,
    "dDurationMs": 2254,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course the gold standard for claiming a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901440,
    "dDurationMs": 6772,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "victory is 5-sigma, which means a 1 in a million chance the result came from an unlucky streak. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908212,
    "dDurationMs": 3368,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That sounds like a high standard, but \nwith so many different experiments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911580,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happening around the world, we do get a lot \nof 3-sigma results that end up being wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916620,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So will this one pan out in the end? Will \nwe find the charm quark in the proton? The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922500,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "melon in the apple box? Well we’ll need to blast a lot more protons to find out, but we may also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get closer to our answer by improving this shiny \nnew tool of machine learning. That technology is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935400,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "developing at a rapid pace, as it’s increasingly \nused across all areas of experimental physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941940,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Computers can come up with and test models \nmuch faster than any physicist, which means the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947160,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists can get on with the more interesting \nwork of interpreting the successful models."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951600,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wherever we land on the question \nof intrinsic proton charm,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954660,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s something charming about \nthis cooperation between artificial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960540,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and natural intelligences working towards the common goal of deciphering the inner workings of space time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967860,
    "dDurationMs": 3624,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, comment responses will return next week. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971484,
    "dDurationMs": 4246,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you really want to watch me try to answer difficult questions "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975730,
    "dDurationMs": 9358,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with mixed success, head over to StarTalk, where my long time colleague Dr. Neil Tyson tries to stump me with some real doozies. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985088,
    "dDurationMs": 2465,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link in the description. And feel free to tell them we sent you."
    } ]
  } ]
}
